Title: A CHRISTMAS CAROL
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. COUNTING HOUSE - DAY

Cold, bleak, biting weather outside. The fog pours in at every chink. SCROOGE, a squeezing, wrenching, grasping old sinner with a pointed nose and wiry chin, sits at his desk. 

In a dismal little cell beyond, a sort of tank, BOB CRATCHIT copies letters. Bob wears a white comforter, trying to warm himself at a single candle.

(SOUND: Scratching of quill pens, wind howling outside)

FRED, Scrooge's nephew, bursts in. His face is ruddy and handsome, his breath smoking in the cold.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug. If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with 'Merry Christmas' on his lips, should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart.

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
I am sorry, with all my heart, to find you so resolute. But I'll keep my Christmas humour to the last. So A Merry Christmas, uncle!

Fred leaves. As he opens the door, MR. POOLE, a portly gentleman, steps in, holding books and papers.

MR. POOLE
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

MR. POOLE
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

MR. POOLE
They are. Still, I wish I could say they were not. A few of us are endeavouring to raise a fund to buy the Poor some meat and drink. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing! I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. I help to support the establishments I have mentioned. Those who are badly off must go there.

MR. POOLE
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

Mr. Poole withdraws. Scrooge glares at Bob Cratchit, who is snuffing his candle.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob promises he will, and runs out into the cold.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

A gloomy suite of rooms in a lowering pile of building up a dark yard. The fog and frost hang heavy. Scrooge approaches the heavy wooden door, key in hand.

(SOUND: Footsteps crunching on frozen cobblestones)

He looks at the large iron knocker. Without undergoing any intermediate process of change, the knocker becomes a face. 

MARLEY'S FACE. Livid, with ghostly spectacles turned up on its forehead. The hair is curiously stirred.

Scrooge stares. It is a knocker again. He turns the key sturdily and walks in.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge sits in his dressing-gown and nightcap before a very low fire, eating a saucepan of gruel. 

(SOUND: A disused bell begins to swing, ringing out loudly, joined by every bell in the house)

The bells cease. 

(SOUND: A clanking noise, deep down below, as if someone were dragging a heavy chain over casks)

The noise comes up the stairs. It comes straight towards his door. It passes through the heavy wood.

MARLEY'S GHOST stands before him. Transparent, bound in a long chain made of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, and heavy steel purses.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much! In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You don't believe in me.

MARLEY'S GHOST
I don't. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

The Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain with a dismal and appalling noise. It takes off the bandage round its head, and its lower jaw drops down upon its breast.

Scrooge falls upon his knees, clasping his hands before his face.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen. If that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death! I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard.

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business. Hear me! My time is nearly gone. I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I--I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One. Look to see me no more!

The Ghost wraps its head, walks backward, and floats out upon the bleak, dark night.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT (LATER)

Scrooge lies in bed. The curtains of his bed are drawn aside by a hand.

(SOUND: A deep, dull, hollow, melancholy bell tolls ONE)

Scrooge starts up. Standing by the bed is the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST. It looks like a child, yet like an old man, wearing a tunic of purest white. From the crown of its head springs a bright clear jet of light. It holds a great extinguisher for a cap.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am! I am the Ghost of Christmas Past. Rise! and walk with me!

The Spirit clasps him gently by the arm. They pass through the wall.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT (PAST)

The warehouse is snug, warm, and bright. A fiddler plays from a lofty desk. 

(MUSIC: A lively, stomping country dance)

OLD FEZZIWIG, in a capacious waistcoat, laughs all over himself. He dances with Mrs. Fezziwig. 

YOUNG SCROOGE, a young man in his prime, watches with joy alongside his fellow apprentice.

The older Scrooge watches his younger self, his heart and soul in the scene.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money. Is that so much that he deserves this praise?

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome. The happiness he gives, is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

Scrooge stops, feeling the Spirit's glance.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What is the matter?

SCROOGE
Nothing. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. PARLOUR - DAY (PAST)

A room, not very large. Young Scrooge, his face now showing the eager, greedy lines of avarice, sits beside BELLE, a fair young girl in a mourning-dress. Tears are in her eyes.

(SOUND: A clock ticking softly, mournfully)

BELLE
It matters little. To you, very little. Another idol has displaced me. A golden one.

YOUNG SCROOGE
This is the even-handed dealing of the world! There is nothing it professes to condemn with such severity as the pursuit of wealth!

BELLE
I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Our contract is an old one. It was made when we were both poor. You are changed. I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

She leaves him.

SCROOGE
Spirit! show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

Scrooge seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down upon the Spirit's head. The light streams from under it, but Scrooge sinks into a heavy sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge awakens. The room is transformed. The walls and ceiling are hung with living green, gleaming with berries. A mighty blaze roars up the chimney. 

(SOUND: Fire roaring and crackling loudly)

Heaped upon the floor are turkeys, geese, game, plum-puddings, and bowls of punch. 

Sitting on this couch is the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT, a jolly Giant in a simple green robe bordered with white fur, wearing a holly wreath.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in! and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! Touch my robe!

Scrooge holds it fast.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

A poor, four-roomed house. MRS. CRATCHIT, dressed poorly but brave in ribbons, lays the cloth. 

(SOUND: Potatoes bubbling in a saucepan, children laughing)

BOB CRATCHIT enters, his threadbare clothes darned up. On his shoulder sits TINY TIM, bearing a little crutch, his limbs supported by an iron frame.

BOB CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

MRS. CRATCHIT
As good as gold.

BOB CRATCHIT
He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day, who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

The family gathers around the table. A small goose is brought out, and a speckled cannon-ball of a pudding. They eat with universal admiration. 

Bob serves hot stuff from a jug into two tumblers and a custard-cup.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Tim sits very close to his father's side. Bob holds his withered little hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head, overcome with penitence.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon. Such an odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man as Mr. Scrooge.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, the children! Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him!

EXT. BLEAK MOOR - NIGHT

A desolate wasteland. The Ghost of Christmas Present has grown older, his hair grey. 

(SOUND: Wind howling bitterly across the rocks)

From the foldings of the Spirit's robe, two children crawl out. Wretched, abject, frightful, hideous. A boy and a girl. IGNORANCE and WANT.

SCROOGE
Spirit! are they yours?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both!

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Ghost vanishes. 

A solemn Phantom, the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME, draped and hooded in a deep black garment, comes like a mist along the ground towards him.

EXT. CITY STREET - DAY (FUTURE)

The Phantom points its outstretched hand. Scrooge follows. 

(SOUND: Coins clinking, the busy murmur of merchants on 'Change)

MR. GRIMES, a great fat man, talks with MR. HAWKINS.

MR. GRIMES
I only know he's dead.

MR. HAWKINS
When did he die?

MR. GRIMES
Last night, I believe. Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

MR. HAWKINS
So I am told. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral, for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it. Suppose we make up a party and volunteer?

They laugh and stroll away. Scrooge looks at the Phantom, trembling.

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT (FUTURE)

A low-browed, beetling shop filled with rusty keys, bones, and greasy offal. OLD JOE smokes his pipe. 

(SOUND: Rats scurrying in the dark corners)

MRS. DILBER, a charwoman, throws a heavy bundle on the floor.

MRS. DILBER
Open that bundle, old Joe. Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did.

Joe drags out a large and heavy roll of dark stuff.

OLD JOE
What do you call this? Bed-curtains! You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

MRS. DILBER
Yes I do. Why not? He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

Scrooge recoils in terror.

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or that dark chamber will be for ever present to me.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY (FUTURE)

Quiet. Very quiet. Mrs. Cratchit and her daughters are engaged in sewing. 

(SOUND: Soft, muffled weeping)

Bob Cratchit enters, his face careworn. He looks at a chair set close beside the fire, empty. He kisses the little face in his memory.

BOB CRATCHIT
My little, little child! My little child!

He breaks down all at once.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - DAY (FUTURE)

Walled in by houses, overrun by grass and weeds. A worthy place. 

(SOUND: A cold, hollow wind whistling through the tombstones)

The Spirit stands among the graves, and points down to One.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The Spirit points downward. Scrooge creeps towards it, trembling. He reads upon the stone of the neglected grave his own name: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

The finger points from the grave to him, and back again. Scrooge falls to his knees, clutching at the Spirit's robe.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Hear me! I am not the man I was. I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He catches the spectral hand. The Phantom shrinks, collapses, and dwindles down into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - DAY

The bedpost is his own. The room is his own. Sunlight floods the room.

(SOUND: Joyous church bells ringing loudly outside, clash, clang, hammer!)

Scrooge scrambles out of bed, tearing at his garments, putting them on upside down, laughing and crying in the same breath.

SCROOGE
They are not torn down, rings and all! I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world. Hallo here! Whoop! Hallo!

He runs to the window and opens it.

EXT. CITY STREET - DAY

Clear, bright, jovial, stirring cold. Golden sunlight. Scrooge walks with his hands behind him, regarding everyone with a delighted smile.

(SOUND: Crunching snow, cheerful crowd chatter)

He beholds Mr. Poole from the day before. Scrooge quickens his pace and takes the old gentleman by both hands.

SCROOGE
My dear sir. How do you do? A merry Christmas to you, sir!

MR. POOLE
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Yes. That is my name. Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness--

Scrooge whispers in his ear.

MR. POOLE
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Will you come and see me?

MR. POOLE
I will!

INT. FRED'S HOUSE - DAY

Fred and his wife are looking at the dining table, spread out in great array. 

(SOUND: Clinking glasses, warm laughter)

Scrooge turns the lock gently and sidles his face in, round the door.

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul! who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred nearly shakes his arm off.

INT. COUNTING HOUSE - DAY

Next morning. The clock strikes nine. Scrooge sits with his door wide open. 

(SOUND: Clock ticking ominously, then a loud thud as Bob drops his hat)

Bob Cratchit rushes into the Tank, full eighteen minutes and a half behind his time. He drives away with his pen.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time. It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend. I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore...

Scrooge leaps from his stool, giving Bob a dig in the waistcoat.

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary! A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you, for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family. Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

A massive, prize Turkey sits on the table. Scrooge sits with the Cratchit family, beaming. Tiny Tim sits beside him, healthy and smiling.

(MUSIC: A warm, swelling orchestral carol)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man, as the good old city knew.

TINY TIM
God bless Us, Every One!

FADE OUT.

THE END
